WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.080
Actually, I was kind of
stirring up my reserve.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.830
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here,
and today I wanna continue talking

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:12.330
about the importance of forgetting
in learning vocabulary and the

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.460
importance of building up a reserve.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.850
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.470
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.160
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:24.990
I do appreciate it.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.259
So I did a video about the
importance of forgetting.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.780
Not only should we not be frustrated
because we forget things because

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:39.239
we're going to forget things, we need
to recognize that the, the act of

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:42.390
forgetting is helping us build a reserve.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.530
And I'm reminded of that because
I'm giving a talk on Monday

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.870
to a group of lawyers in Kyiv,
Ukraine about language learning.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.660
I was contacted through, uh, LinkedIn
probably as a result of the videos

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.129
that I did, uh, telling people that,
um, Ukrainians have free access

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:02.730
to LingQ, whether they're refugees
in other countries or in Ukraine.

00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:07.710
By the way, the same offer is
available to other refugees.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.740
They could be Afghans,
Ethiopians, you name it.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.350
Through that, so I was
contacted by this person.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.400
I'm gonna give a talk and I'm gonna
talk a bit about my experience

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.110
in learning languages, obviously.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.160
And it just so happened that in
order to prepare for my talk to

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.490
these Ukrainians where I'm gonna
speak in both Ukrainian and English,

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.940
I decided to refresh my Ukrainian.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.929
And what struck me was that as I
was going through my mini stories in

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.830
Ukrainian, you know, learning some of
the basic, you know, high frequency

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:42.869
verbs and the patterns of Ukrainian
triggered the memory of other bits

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.880
of Ukrainian, other expressions.

00:01:44.884 --> 00:01:51.020
So by working my mini stories, which I
have done many, many times, actually,

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:54.389
I was kind of stirring up my reserve.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.589
And so I think it's very important
that when we learn a language, and

00:01:58.589 --> 00:02:02.339
this is what I said in that previous
video, we shouldn't sort of be too

00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:06.660
narrowly focused on trying to make
sure that we can retrieve things, that

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:10.590
we can remember things that, and, and
therefore we get upset if we don't

00:02:10.595 --> 00:02:12.185
remember or we can't find the word.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:18.470
That may not matter that much, at least in
the short run or even in the medium term.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.690
What we wanna do is build up our reserve.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:26.750
And so then I thought about how I
use LingQ, and I realized that really

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:32.540
the LingQs that I create, which
are my yellow words, is my reserve.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.519
These are words that I have met.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.100
I know I've met them before
because they're yellow.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.170
They're no longer blue
but they aren't yet white.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:46.440
A white word is one which I feel I
know, at least in a given context.

00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:54.090
It doesn't mean that I know all possible,
you know, interpretations or uses of that

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.050
word, nor which other words that word
is used together with, but in a given

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.615
context it doesn't give me any trouble.

00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:02.924
I understand that word in that context.

00:03:02.924 --> 00:03:06.045
I get meaning out of that
sentence or that paragraph.

00:03:06.195 --> 00:03:07.155
That means it's known.

00:03:07.665 --> 00:03:08.415
So that's white.

00:03:08.475 --> 00:03:11.865
But the yellow words are words that, I
see them and I still don't know them.

00:03:12.075 --> 00:03:15.525
So they're the words that I have perhaps
certainly have seen, but I've forgotten.

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:18.675
Maybe at one point I knew them and
I've moved them back to yellow.

00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:21.495
These are words in my reserve.

00:03:21.675 --> 00:03:23.805
These are my forgotten words.

00:03:24.075 --> 00:03:30.170
The creative forgetting  l'oubli
créateur as Proust said, and it's

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:32.120
important to have a lot of these words.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.340
So the known words, the white words,
the known words, are ones that I

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.120
can retrieve at least passively in
terms of reading and understanding.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:45.930
So if I look at my statistics in
a language like Ukrainian, where

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.800
I benefit from having learned
Russian and Czech and so forth.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.300
So I have a lot of known words,
almost 60,000 known words.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.730
Not so many yellow words, and many of
those yellow words are in fact words that

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.220
I know that I haven't yet moved to known.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.290
So there the reserve and the
words that I can retrieve are

00:04:06.295 --> 00:04:08.030
kind of similar in number.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:13.100
Uh, if I were to go in French, for
example, and if I were to study French

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:17.240
at LingQ, I would find that I would have
very, you know, a far smaller number

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.800
in the sort of unretrievable reserve.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.370
In other words, yellow words that are
still yellow that haven't become white.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.920
On the other hand, if I go to Arabic
and Persian, I see a different story.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.590
I see that in Arabic going from memory.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:38.549
Here I have 60,000 saved LingQs.

00:04:39.405 --> 00:04:43.785
My reserve is enormous, but
I only know 20,000 of those.

00:04:43.785 --> 00:04:47.055
And if I, having left Arabic,
maybe that number is even

00:04:47.055 --> 00:04:51.585
smaller because the reserve, the
forgotten words is even greater.

00:04:51.855 --> 00:04:54.485
But eventually I will activate those.

00:04:55.425 --> 00:05:00.705
Uh, the words that are in my reserve in
Persian, the ratio is also three to one.

00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:05.865
I have 30,000 saving LingQs, but
only 12,000 or so known words.

00:05:06.315 --> 00:05:08.745
Bearing in mind that these,
the status here can change.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:10.935
You can have something that's
known that then goes back to

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:12.375
unknown and goes back to known.

00:05:12.675 --> 00:05:17.305
But the idea is that when you are at
the stage where I am in Arabic and

00:05:17.305 --> 00:05:23.115
Persian I have the, my reserve is three
times larger than the words that I can

00:05:23.175 --> 00:05:25.635
at least understand and that's fine.

00:05:25.935 --> 00:05:29.055
And I think the big message that
I wanna sort of put across here

00:05:29.055 --> 00:05:34.155
is don't worry about forgetting,
considering consider forgetting to

00:05:34.155 --> 00:05:35.715
be an important part of learning.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:37.820
You are building up your reserve.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.734
You need a large reserve so that
eventually you can activate these words

00:05:42.794 --> 00:05:47.294
and have a, a functioning, you know,
a large functioning vocabulary so that

00:05:47.299 --> 00:05:50.775
you can understand movies, you can have
intelligent conversations with people,

00:05:50.895 --> 00:05:53.234
you can move in the direction of fluency.

00:05:53.354 --> 00:05:57.075
So forgetting and building up
your reserve is an important

00:05:57.075 --> 00:05:59.265
part of moving towards fluency.

00:05:59.354 --> 00:06:01.994
And I'll leave you with a couple of
videos that I've done in the past

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:06.725
about memory and remembering and how
that effects uh, language learning.

00:06:06.785 --> 00:06:08.525
So thank you very much for listening.

00:06:08.525 --> 00:06:09.065
Bye for now.

